+
+Destructive includes overwriting existing settings, reallocating live traffic, replacing prices or availability, enabling advertising spend, and irreversible sends such as feedback or webhook retries. A reversible change can still overwrite existing state. Generic API tools advertise the effects of every operation they can reach; POST alone does not mean an operation is additive, and a POST lookup can be read-only.
+
+`query` forces SQL to read-only mode, but its backend can provision analytics credentials and reconcile access policies before running SQL. Generic `get` can reach the same analytics endpoint. Those setup effects are separate from the SQL's read-only guarantee.
+
+The hints your client sees depend on the CLI version deployed by the MCP server. Keep destructive approvals enabled and review the tool's description and inputs.
 
 The CLI asks before deleting, archiving, or anything else that can't be undone. A tool doesn't ask: your MCP client's approval prompt is the confirmation, so keep approvals on for destructive tools.
 
